WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:12.400
I think nobody will be surprised if I say
that immigration is one of the most important

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.450
issues in international politics...

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:20.300
And certainly, both in Europe and the United
States, lately everything related to this

00:00:20.300 --> 00:01:23.100
issue takes over most of the news and front
covers.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.440
Without going any further, here in VISUALPOLITIK
we’ve already spoken a few times about this

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:33.799
subject… and we’ll soon post a special
video, first on Patreon and then here on YouTube,

00:01:33.799 --> 00:01:39.250
commenting – and dismantling – many of
the myths, and half-truths that exist on this

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:40.250
subject.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:41.250
By the way… before we continue.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.250
Thank you.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:46.760
Thank each and every one of you for your support,
we just surpassed the 480.000 subscriber mark.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.259
So really, thank you very much...

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:52.600
but, before proceeding with the video, I want
to give a special thanks to all of you who

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.740
are supporting us on Patreon.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:59.210
Thanks to you we’ve already managed to publish
50% more videos, and now we’re preparing

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:02.979
many surprises and, of course, all the other
things that we’ve promised you.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:08.149
But having said that, let's continue.

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:21.401
My friends, this time I’d like to share
some reflections with you, some ideas... which

00:02:21.401 --> 00:02:24.900
I think we should keep in mind when talking
about immigration.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.980
This is particularly important if we consider
that lately mistrust, fear and anti-immigration

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:33.269
political speeches are very, very much alive.

00:02:33.269 --> 00:02:35.370
(“"Get out of my country.”

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:40.380
I can still hear that sentence with absolute
clarity, as if it occupies a specific place

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.380
in my mind.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:42.380
It’s a scar.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.380
Deep within.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.750
The man who said “Get out of my country”
was a Trump supporter.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:51.810
I know this because he was wearing a pin identifying
the then candidate on one of his lapels.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:55.069
But most of all, I know this because of the
way he said it to me.

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:58.200
He looked me straight in the eyes, pointed
a finger at me, and shouted.”

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.330
- Jorge Ramos, journalist of Univision and
U.S citizen who was born in Mexico.)

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:08.209
One of the most typical and popular arguments
of those who oppose immigration is stating

00:03:08.209 --> 00:03:12.390
that immigrants take our jobs and take the
money they make back to their countries...

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:18.080
well, as long as we don’t talk about, Messi,
Cristiano Ronaldo, Nadal or Rolling Stones...

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.390
There, of course, there’s no problem.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.750
A bit hypocritical, don’t you think?

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.379
Now, why do people decide to emigrate?

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:30.400
Who are these people who pack their bags and
leave their land?

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.190
Who benefits from all this movement?

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:36.140
In general, why is there such a negative perception
of this phenomenon?

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:41.790
In this video I’ll try to answer some of
these questions… but first, I think we should

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.810
take a look at how it all began…

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:45.760
When, why and where?

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:53.900
By the way, what would you say if I told you
that, in some way, you’re also an immigrant?

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:59.409
Listen up.

00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:04.930
(THE ORIGIN)

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.770
From the very beginnings of humanity, people
moved.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:12.810
Basically their motivation was to find food...
which forced them to move continuously from

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:13.939
one place to another.

00:04:13.939 --> 00:04:16.220
Of course, back then borders didn’t exist.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:23.710
The only limitation to moving from one place
to another was how far we could travel.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:29.681
But, of course, all this began to change when
"modern" societies emerged, that is, when

00:04:29.681 --> 00:04:34.550
communities settled in a particular place
where they worked to produce food, tools and

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:39.990
everything they needed to trade and live.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:44.169
Now... one thing we can ask ourselves is...

00:04:44.169 --> 00:04:48.539
Did the migration phenomenon disappear with
this new way of life?

00:04:48.539 --> 00:04:52.040
Well... not at all.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:57.840
See, once the first "modern" societies established
themselves in certain places and governments

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:05.250
began to emerge back around 3000 BC, migration
didn’t stop, although motivations did change...

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:10.830
Two large migration groups were created, so
to speak, two groups with very different motivations:

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:16.230
on the one hand, conquering, and on the other,
searching for opportunities.

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:24.000
The first, the conquests, were movements carried
out by societies that had achieved a higher

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.310
level of development and who wanted to take
advantage of it to gain wealth from the people

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:34.510
they conquered.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:39.090
This involved a migratory movement from the
most developed countries to the less developed

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:43.920
countries to monopolize their resources.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:48.270
Although this phenomenon wasn’t exclusive
to any continent, the case that we all know

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:54.120
best, is perhaps that of the Europeans who
managed to control almost the entire world,

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.180
starting with America.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:59.770
Of course, this type of migration doesn’t
look anything like the one we have today.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:01.940
The second type of migration does.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:06.699
We’re talking about the peaceful mobilization
of millions of people who seek to escape their

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:13.370
territory of origin to reach another territory
where they can find new and better opportunities.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:19.139
Therefore, in this case, their drive is to
search for economic improvements, and sometimes,

00:06:19.139 --> 00:06:22.310
also to escape political and religious persecution.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:27.020
Therefore, in general, we’re talking about
peaceful migrations from the least developed

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:33.949
countries to the most developed countries,
the opposite of conquests.

00:06:33.949 --> 00:06:40.419
Well, the first large case happened at the
end of the Middle Ages, when cities grew and

00:06:40.419 --> 00:06:44.069
people began to leave the countryside to move
to these cities.

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:49.960
In any case, this was a very slow process,
but everything changed with the arrival of

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:54.060
the Industrial Revolution.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:07:02.139
It’s estimated that between 1850 and 1930
almost 60 million Europeans – which was

00:07:02.139 --> 00:07:06.501
a huge percentage of the population of that
time – emigrated to the Americas, to countries

00:07:06.501 --> 00:07:13.710
like the United States, Canada, Brazil and
Argentina.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:17.930
That is to say, despite all the political
discourse against immigration, the truth is

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:21.569
that Europe has a well-defined history as
an emigrant territory:

00:07:21.569 --> 00:07:26.759
Europeans, first, emigrated massively from
the countryside to the cities, then they moved

00:07:26.759 --> 00:07:31.949
around the world and now, in our times, millions
and millions of Europeans have left their

00:07:31.949 --> 00:07:36.470
land to live in another European Union country.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.780
And what about the United States...

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:43.100
almost all of its population – if not all
– comes from other countries or lives in

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:47.669
territories that belonged to other countries,
as is the case of the lands of California,

00:07:47.669 --> 00:07:51.539
New Mexico and Texas, which historically belonged
to Mexico.

00:07:51.539 --> 00:07:56.430
So... somehow we’re all descendants of immigrants.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:01.550
In fact, if we take the current anti-immigration
demonstrations to the extreme, we could state

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:05.479
that we are all immigrants in the regions
in which we live.

00:08:05.479 --> 00:08:06.479
Now...

00:08:06.479 --> 00:08:09.730
The question we can ask is, what are the consequences
of all this?

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:13.449
Could it have something to do with the fact
that most of the richest and most developed

00:08:13.449 --> 00:08:20.600
countries in the world have been so exposed
to the migratory process?

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:24.740
Listen up.

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:27.919
(WHAT HISTORY TELLS US)

00:08:27.919 --> 00:08:36.190
We’ll say it loud and clear: most immigrants
share a common goal: to achieve a better life

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:37.190
and progress.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:41.040
It’s true, some people with deep pockets
or a good standard of living move from their

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:46.390
country to grow professionally, live new experiences
or just live differently.

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:53.530
These immigrants who usually arrive with a
full wallet almost never awaken anyone's mistrust.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:59.830
However, the same doesn’t apply to those
people who are escaping hunger, misery, all

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:05.500
kinds of political, religious and social persecutions...
that is, immigrants who arrive with an empty

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:07.590
wallet in search of new opportunities.

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:12.080
This type of immigration is by far the most
important and, of course, the one that generates

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:16.720
the greatest mistrust, hostility and criticism.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:17.750
However...

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:21.220
this immigration is almost identical to our
ancestors’: we’re talking about people

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:26.380
who are risking their lives and who decided
to leave everything behind - Which isn’t

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:27.560
easy.

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:28.940
And all for what?

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:34.500
Same as always: to achieve a better future,
to work and prosper in a lawful way

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:40.360
(“Little is more extraordinary than the
decision to migrate ... the accumulation of

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:45.380
emotions and thoughts which finally leads
a family to say farewell to a community where

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:51.080
it has lived for centuries, to abandon old
ties and familiar landmarks, and to sail across

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:56.950
dark seas to a strange land” John F. Kennedy,
President of the United States of America.)

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:59.090
Tell me, who built America?

00:09:59.090 --> 00:10:04.340
Who promoted the extraordinary economic takeoff
that Argentina had at the end of the 19th

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:05.340
century?

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:09.340
Well, folks, it was the immigrants and their
descendants.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:13.370
Why do we think that things will be different
today?

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:16.010
But of course...

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:19.860
when economic data shows that immigration
drives growth and development due to higher

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:22.660
employment levels, entrepreneurship and productivity...

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:25.080
then anti-immigration critics...

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:26.500
make up many other excuses.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:33.820
We’re going to talk about them, one by one
in a future video, with data in hand... but

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:39.570
today I’d like to end this video reflection
with one of the most important myths: fear

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:48.150
and crime.

00:10:48.150 --> 00:11:05.550
(THE GREAT CRIME MYTH)

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:10.550
One of the most widely differing myths is
that immigrants, especially undocumented immigrants,

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:15.450
the so-called "illegal immigrants", are the
ones responsible for most crimes and delinquency.

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:16.860
Well... you know what?

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:18.910
The data says the opposite.

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:23.430
It's true, there are legal and illegal immigrants
who commit crimes.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:27.620
However, let's take, for example, a look at
what happens in the country with the most

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:30.950
immigrants in the world: United States.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:37.010
See, between 1990 and 2013, the number of
undocumented immigrants, known as illegal

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:41.080
immigrants, went from 3 and a half million
people to approximately 11 million.

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:45.450
However, in that same period, according to
the FBI, the number of violent crimes dropped

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:47.460
by 48%.

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.330
Surprised?

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:54.490
Well, if we take the crime data into account
you shouldn’t be.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:12:00.070
See, according to an Immigration Policy Center
study, only 1.6% of immigrant men aged 18

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:01.760
to 39 end up in prison.

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:07.340
Among those born in the United States, the
data is 3.3%, more than double.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:13.320
Check out this graph on crime levels in Texas,
one of the states with the most legal and

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:17.560
illegal immigrants.

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:25.600
Yes, we can agree that we need to re-order
the migratory processes...

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:30.760
But stigmatizing immigrants and outlawing
their condition only favors the human trafficking

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:31.760
mafias.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:36.300
The evidence is clear, immigrants try to reach
countries with higher levels of freedom and

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:41.560
more open economic systems, places, in short,
where they can work and enjoy life without

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:43.260
fear.

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:49.540
Therefore, perhaps a good measure, and maybe
the only one, to control the migratory flow

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:54.420
is to promote trade, private investment and
democratic institutions in the countries people

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:55.420
are leaving from.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:58.730
But meanwhile, developed countries can take
advantage of immigration, in the same way

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:01.530
as other societies did throughout history.

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:08.040
Immigrants, my friends, want to work and prosper.

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:11.730
